#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# polar_plot

#% text
# `polar_plot(r, [theta, a, b])` draws the curve whose distance from the origin is r at the angle theta, for theta from a to b.

#% text
# `polar_plot([r1, r2, ...], [theta, a, b])` draws several such curves in one picture.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The value is `image("path")`, a PNG file with SVG and PDF copies next to it.

#% item
# Angles are in radians, measured anticlockwise from the positive x axis; a negative r lies on the opposite side of the origin.

#% item
# The axes cross at the origin and use the same unit, so circles are drawn round.

#% item
# The options `x_label`, `y_label`, `legend`, `legend_position`, `colors`, `line_style` and `plot_range` work as for `plot`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A cardioid:

#% cell
polar_plot(1 + cos(theta), [theta, 0, 2pi])
#% output
#= image("polar_plot.files/polar_plot-1.png")

#% text
# A rose with three petals:

#% cell
polar_plot(sin(3theta), [theta, 0, pi])
#% output
#= image("polar_plot.files/polar_plot-2.png")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A circle and a spiral together:

#% cell
polar_plot([1, theta/(2pi)], [theta, 0, 4pi], legend: ["r = 1", "spiral"])
#% output
#= image("polar_plot.files/polar_plot-3.png")

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The same curve as a parametric plot of r cos(theta) and r sin(theta):

#% cell
parametric_plot([(1 + cos(t))*cos(t), (1 + cos(t))*sin(t)], [t, 0, 2pi])
#% output
#= image("polar_plot.files/polar_plot-4.png")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `parametric_plot`, `plot`
